---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../data/site';
import { measurements, threads, environment, fine, pct } from '../data/measurements';
import TierStack from '../components/charts/TierStack.astro';
import FormatFlow from '../components/charts/FormatFlow.astro';
import PreemptHistogram from '../components/charts/PreemptHistogram.astro';

const cfs = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs schedstat')!;
const perfRun = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs perf run time')!;
const wait = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'off-CPU vs perf wait time')!;
---

<Base
  title="The product — PACE"
  description="What PACE is: a C and C++ counter, an open-source analyzer, and the trace formats both of them understand."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">The product</div>
      <h1>One measurement, three ways to consume it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        PACE is not two products that happen to ship together, and the components are not separately
        useful on their own. The library is the thing that measures; the analyzer is the proof that
        the measurement is real; the service is the analyser without the build step. They share one
        model of time, and the model is a single line of arithmetic.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href={href('install/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-download-simple"></i>
          Install it
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={href('service/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-activity"></i>
          Try the hosted analyzer
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The model</div>
      <h2>Four numbers per sample, and one equation that has to hold</h2>
      <p>
        A sample is a window of wall time plus the three ways a thread can spend it. The arithmetic
        is not a statistical model fitted to the data — it is four measurements of the same window,
        and the analyzer rejects a trace where they do not add up.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-clock"></i> wall_ns</h3>
          <p>
            The window, from <span class="mono">CLOCK_MONOTONIC</span>. Measured independently by the
            verifier as well as by PACE, so the two can be compared:
            {' '}{fine(measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'wall vs CLOCK_MONOTONIC')!.median)} median
            disagreement.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-play-circle"></i> on_cpu_ns</h3>
          <p>
            The thread was on a core. Cross-checked against the kernel's own cfs accounting at
            {' '}{fine(cfs.median)} median, and against
            <span class="mono">perf sched timehist</span> at {fine(perfRun.median)}.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-pause-circle"></i> preempted_ns</h3>
          <p>
            Runnable, and the scheduler had other work. This is the run-queue delay, which only Linux
            reports directly. It is the number no CPU-time counter can produce.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-hourglass"></i> blocked_ns</h3>
          <p>
            Neither running nor runnable. Cross-checked against perf's wait-time column at
            {' '}{fine(wait.median)} median, which is the tightest of the off-CPU comparisons.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Architecture</div>
      <h2>Three tiers, and the one at the bottom is the product</h2>
      <p>
        The design decision that shapes everything else: a profiler vendor cannot require root from
        its users. So the portable tier needs nothing at all, and the two richer tiers attach to it
        when the machine allows.
      </p>
      <TierStack />

      <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Platform support, stated exactly</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Platform</th>
              <th>Tier 0</th>
              <th>Three-way split</th>
              <th>Tier 1</th>
              <th>Tier 2</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Linux</td>
              <td class="ok">yes</td>
              <td class="ok">yes — run-queue delay from procfs</td>
              <td class="ok">with CAP_PERFMON</td>
              <td class="ok">with privilege and BTF</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Windows</td>
              <td class="ok">yes</td>
              <td class="dim">no — on-CPU vs blocked only</td>
              <td class="dim">no</td>
              <td class="dim">no</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>macOS</td>
              <td class="ok">yes</td>
              <td class="dim">no — on-CPU vs blocked only</td>
              <td class="dim">no</td>
              <td class="dim">no</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">A dash is not a zero</div>
        <p>
          On Windows and macOS PACE reports on-CPU against blocked and marks the split as
          <em>unavailable</em>. It does not report a confident zero for the preempted bucket, because
          zero is a measurement and there it would be a false one. The test suite asserts the flags
          are clear.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:36px">The kernel has to be collecting it, too</h3>
      <p>
        Even on Linux the three-way split needs the kernel's scheduler accounting, which a
        distribution kernel may leave switched off. PACE detects that case and reports the split as
        unavailable rather than as zero, for the same reason. The verification machine ran kernel
        {' '}<span class="mono">{String(environment.uname).split(' ')[2]}</span> with
        {' '}<span class="mono">perf_event_paranoid={environment.kernel_perf_event_paranoid}</span>,
        which is the strict default — and tier 0 worked anyway, which is the point of tier 0.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Interfaces</div>
      <h2>One C ABI, and a trace format if you would rather not embed</h2>
      <FormatFlow />
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-brackets-curly"></i> In-process</h3>
          <pre><code>{`pace_config cfg;
pace_config_init(&cfg);

pace_session *session;
pace_session_create(&cfg, &session);

pace_thread *thread;
pace_session_register_thread(session, &thread);

pace_sample sample;
pace_session_sample(session, thread, &sample);

// sample.on_cpu_ns
// sample.preempted_ns
// sample.blocked_ns
// sample.wall_ns
// sample.source`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            One call per sample, no allocation on the hot path, and a struct with a
            <span class="mono">struct_size</span> field so the ABI can grow without breaking a
            consumer that was built against an older header.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-file-text"></i> As a trace</h3>
          <p>
            An optional JSONL writer emits Chrome Trace Event records, so a vendor's existing viewer
            can already open the file. The open-source analyzer adds the attribution report on top of
            it, and <span class="mono">pace verify</span> can gate a CI job on the invariant.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stable error codes for the agent-ready path:
            <span class="mono">io-error</span>, <span class="mono">parse-error</span>,
            <span class="mono">format-unknown</span>, <span class="mono">empty-trace</span>,
            <span class="mono">unsupported-output</span>, <span class="mono">write-error</span>,
            <span class="mono">invalid-argument</span>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Scale</div>
      <h2>What happens when the trace is bigger than memory</h2>
      <p>
        The analyzer folds each sample into its thread's aggregate as it reads, so memory is
        proportional to the number of threads and not to the number of samples. On a trace of about a
        hundred thousand samples the streaming path used 3.4 MB against 16.8 MB for the retaining
        path, and the two produce identical totals. The summarised path checks the invariant as it
        reads, so the report never says "exact" for a trace it did not check.
      </p>
      <PreemptHistogram />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">What it is not</div>
      <h2>The scope, in the product's own words</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-stack"></i> Not a sampler</h3>
          <p>Stack unwinding, symbolisation and flame graphs are the vendor's product. PACE supplies the attribution those profilers cannot make.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cpu"></i> Not a GPU or cache profiler</h3>
          <p>Different counters, different buyer, different product.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cloud-slash"></i> Not a continuous production agent</h3>
          <p>PACE measures a window you choose. Continuous always-on profiling is a different requirement set.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The whole measured position, including the parts that disagree with it, is in
        {' '}<a href={href('research/')}>the research section</a>. The licence terms are
        {' '}on <a href={href('license/')}>the licence page</a>, and the source is
        {' '}<a href={site.repo}>on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
